// 0.0.236 A7: a string passed through an UNTYPED parameter prints a POINTER,
// not text. Same root-cause family as A6 (which was the f64 case): the parameter
// slot carries no type, the callee's `print` therefore takes the integer path,
// and the user sees a 20-digit number where text was expected.
//
// Verified with the 0.0.236 compiler:
//   - this file prints two large integers (pointer/bit-pattern values)
//   - annotating `fn show(tag: str, x: str)` makes the SAME program print
//     "global     : GLOBAL" etc. correctly
//   - both forms compile WITHOUT complaint, so the failure is silent
//
// Every TYPED string path was checked and is correct: global, local, struct
// field, array element, returned value, and a returned value held in a local.
// So A7's original wording ("other string paths can still emit a pointer") does
// NOT reproduce; the untyped parameter is the live case.
//
// When fixed, promote this into a gate row (red-first by construction).
fn make_one() -> str {
    return "GLOBAL"
}

fn show(tag, x) {
    print(tag)
    print(x)
    print("\n")
}

fn main() {
    show("untyped tag: ", make_one())
    show("untyped lit: ", "TEXT")
    exit(0)
}
